NOTES_NV = {
    7: 'When an individual can buy several units of an item, the seller can think about a advanced pricing strategies. This is the case of continuous choice as opposed to binary choice (buy/don’t buy). Diminishing marginal personal value comes into play – the value of each subsequent unit falls.',
    8: 'Ask in class if this is a price searcher or price taker\n\nThe dilemma of pricing is that if you charge a high price to capture a lot of value, you exclude many from wanting to buy the product, namely the hordes of those with valuations for the product that are lower than price. The alternative is to go with volume, but then you have to charge a low price and you give away a lot of consumer surplus, leave a lot of surplus on the table which you do not capture. There is a dilemma. Today we will see how to escape the dilemma through price discrimination.',
    9: 'Consumers are better off under resale: low-price consumers can resell their purchases at a profit; high-price consumers can buy product at a lower price than the firm would charge\nFirms are not better off, since only selling at one price, the lower price!',
    14: 'In an ideal world you would like to charge individual prices to mop up all the surplus – to capture all the area beneath the Demand curve in the form of corporate revenue. This type of customized pricing represents the ideal. Customized to what? To the buyers’ valuations! This is difficult to do in practice for a variety of reasons: e.g., arbitrage, legal restrictions.',
    15: 'In an ideal world you would like to charge individual prices to mop up all the surplus – to capture all the area beneath the Demand curve in the form of corporate revenue. This type of customized pricing represents the ideal. Customized to what? To the buyers’ valuations! This is difficult to do in practice for a variety of reasons: e.g., arbitrage, legal restrictions.',
    19: 'We reintroduce segment pricing, a concept already introduced in week 2 when we discussed Mercedes’s optimal pricing in the US and Germany, based on different elasticities.\n\xa0\nAt the car wash, senior citizens get a discount. This is price discrimination based on age. Why do firms select different prices for different segments? We talked about incentives to discriminate when different market segments exhibit different demand elasticities. You want to equate marginal revenue across segments, assuming marginal cost is equal across segments. The implication of this is that you want to charge a lower price for the more elastic segment. \n\xa0\nSo why are senior citizens more price sensitive?\n\xa0\nGoing to the car wash is a time consuming activity. It can take 40 minutes, sometimes more for an upscale treatment. Senior citizens can afford to drive around longer for a better deal. Opportunity cost of time. They have lower income, so they are more price sensitive The opportunity cost of time for a senior person is much lower than say for a professional. A professional is concerned about the value of time. Senior citizens don’t care so much about the time component of the cost of the car wash, so they are more sensitive to price.',
    20: 'Why would you charge lower price to students? lower incomes and more free time. As a result, they are more price-elastic and it is optimal to offer them discounts.\n\nImportant: you need to “verify” that they are indeed students.',
    21: 'Disneyland and Taj Mahal: lower prices for Socal/Indian residents compared to tourists. Why? Tourists have lower elasticity of demand than locals that have more easily available alternatives (and in the Indian case, lower income). Generalized phenomenon in tourism – in India airlines used to charge higher fares to tourists.\n\nAnother Example: US drugs cheaper in Canada than in USA. There is more discretion as to price in the US because there is less collective bargaining between public entities and pharmaceutical companies for lower prices. This creates incentives for transshipping of drugs – parallel imports to take advantage of arbitrage opportunities.',
    22: 'One can go directly to the website and look for favorite goods. I use coffee moka bialetti:\nhttps://camelcamelcamel.com/',
    23: 'For next slide: the price of Lodine for humans is three times the price for animals',
    26: 'For next slide: the price of Lodine for humans is three times the price for animals',
    28: 'For next slide: the price of Lodine for humans is three times the price for animals',
    34: 'An example of Second Degree Price Discrimination: Versioning\n\xa0\nWe will go in greater depth into one form of second degree price discrimination which is versioning. Versioning is incentive compatible and accomplishes everything that price discrimination accomplishes. Consumers who buy at a high price do so willingly. The key to understand this is that the version priced at a premium has to have features that generate some value to the consumer, but the cost differential to the company is minor relative to the price differential (otherwise there is no real discrimination on price if the surplus captured is the same across segments).\n\xa0\nThe perfect example is Microsoft: offers Windows Professional and Windows Home Edition. Windows Pro has more features than Home edition but they are otherwise close substitutes. The marginal cost of producing each is identical (and approximately zero) – in fact it may cost the company something to actually differentiate the products. Sometimes it costs nothing to make something better but you don’t want to do it: why doesn’t Microsoft offer the full version of Windows to all their customers since the MC of doing so is zero? Because they would then join the markets, and forego the benefits of price discrimination. You add features to the price insensitive segment so you can charge them more.',
    38: 'The tsunami lapped the foundations of the halls of Congress Wednesday when Sen. Elizabeth Warren, D-Mass., issued from her official U.S. Senate X/Twitter account:\n“Wendy’s is planning to try out ‘surge pricing’ — that means you could pay more for your lunch, even if the cost to Wendy’s stays exactly the same. It’s price gouging plain and simple, and American families have had enough.”',
    41: 'Another Example: Fun Film sold at Price Costco. This is a classic example. Kodak was selling their premium Royal Gold film. Fuji and others started competing. Kodak wanted to protect the low end of their market. They could have lowered the price on Royal Gold, but then you give up capturing the surplus at the high end. So they introduced a new brand, FunFilm, offered only at discount stores like Sam’s Club, Costco, in bulk. This is where price sensitive customers go. It turns out the two films were chemically identical. The packaging and bulk sales aspect were different but the actual product was the same. This is a classic B-school case.',
    42: 'Checkout stand coupons: Nobody wants to spend money unnecessarily, but it takes time to sort through the coupons. Those who have nothing else to do on a Sunday morning, i.e. those with a low opportunity cost of time, who are price sensitive, will take the time to cut the coupons. This is an ingenious scheme to charge higher price to the low elasticity customers to capture more consumer surplus.',
    46: 'With MC=0, people buy to where the value is zero. You want to capture the entire area under the demand curve. How much does the consumer pay? For a flat fee of $400, he would be just willing to purchase the bundle of 20 units. The company doubles their contribution and extracts all the surplus. \n\nEquivalent pricing schemes: A sliding scale discount pricing scheme amounts to the same thing – charge lower and lower amounts for each subsequent unit sold. Here you march the consumer down the demand curve and you charge successively lower prices that allow you to capture all the surplus. The quantity discounts come from the seller’s realization that the buyer has a downward sloping demand curve.',
    47: 'This chart represent the pricing strategy of Netflix, as an example of versioning. They have three plans, from basic to premium. The chart on the right represents the change in strategy that Netflix followed over time\nThis is a very good video on increase in price in 2017, where they also discuss previous price increases and the backlash associated with them\nhttps://finance.yahoo.com/news/netflix-price-hikes-wont-hurt-subscriber-growth-211526185.html',
    49: 'Only 65 customers bought the flat rate pass',
    62: 'Note that the pricing strategy can be also be expressed as 100 at $25, 125 at $30, 175 at 35$. Consumers will opt to purchase 175 at $35 and Walmart will still increase its producer surplus',
    66: 'Attempts to extract consumer surplus',
    67: 'Attempts to extract consumer surplus',
    68: 'Combination of block pricing and versioning',
    69: 'Attempts to extract consumer surplus',
    70: 'Attempts to extract consumer surplus',
    71: 'The experiment was as follows, \n- In one case consumers saw two options at 125 one including only print and one including print and web, and one at $59.\n- In the other treatment consumer were given two options: $59 and $125 for print and web. \nWhen people saw the three options 84% of them chose print and web and only 16% the cheapest option. Their attention went to the comparison between the two options at 125.\nIn the experiment with two options only 32% of them chose the expensive option, most of the (68% chose the $59 option)\n\nExplanation: https://cxl.com/blog/pricing-experiments-you-might-not-know-but-can-learn-from/',
    72: 'Explanation: https://cxl.com/blog/pricing-experiments-you-might-not-know-but-can-learn-from/',
    73: "Price framing is the optimization of how to present prices, services, and products in the most appealing way. It's not changing the prices of your products or services but the way they are exhibited in order to encourage consumers to make more purchases.",
}

